WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.285
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.275 --> 00:00:06.900
SPEAKER 1: Cloud
Shell Editor gives you

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.150
the power to easily write
code from your web browser

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.680
and deploy it to the Cloud.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.540
In previous videos,
we learned how

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.420
to deploy applications to
Cloud Run and Google Kubernetes

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.500
Engine.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.780
We also learned about the
Cloud code integration,

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.660
and how it provides convenient
tools to debug applications

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.300
and use Google Cloud APIs.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.513
But what if you want to
collaborate with teammates?

00:00:26.513 --> 00:00:27.930
Cloud Shell Editor
has you covered

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.050
with version control and
support for multiple projects.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.470
Let's take a look
and learn more.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.190
First, visit
ide.cloud.google.com

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:38.250
to launch Cloud Shell Editor.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.160
Cloud Shell Editor
gives you the power

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.860
to manage multiple
projects as Workspaces.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:44.870
A Workspace usually
represents the root folder

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:47.630
of your project, as well as
project-specific configuration

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:48.800
files.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:52.430
With Workspaces, you can
specify persistent settings, run

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.950
and debug configurations
and UI states

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:57.080
for all the folders
in your Workspace.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.290
Here we can see some recent
Workspaces for this user.

00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:01.700
To open the Home
Workspace that's

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:06.320
created for you automatically,
click Open Home Workspace.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:08.000
This opens up the
Home Workspace,

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.920
displaying all of
its folders and files

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.360
in the Explorer panel.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.220
To create a new
Workspace, we'll first

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.370
want to create a new directory
to use as our Workspace.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:20.270
Right-click on the Explorer
panel and select New Folder.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.970
Give your new folder
a name and click OK.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:28.490
Let's also go ahead and add
a new file to this folder.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.140
Once your directory
is ready, you

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:33.380
can open it as a new Workspace
with File, Open, Workspace.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:39.290
You can also configure settings
for your individual Workspaces.

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:42.590
Settings are accessible by
opening File, Settings, Open

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.720
Preferences.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.390
Here you can adjust two
different types of settings--

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:49.850
User settings and
Workspace settings.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.860
User settings apply
to all your instances

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:55.040
of Cloud Shell Editor, while
Workspace settings apply only

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.320
to your currently
active Workspace.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.160
Closing a Workspace is easy.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.410
Just select File,
Close, Workspace.

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:06.507
This will bring you back to the
Cloud Shell Editor homepage.

00:02:06.507 --> 00:02:08.840
Now let's learn about how to
use source control with Git

00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:10.250
from Cloud Shell Editor.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:14.270
Click Open, Home, Workspace
to open your Home Workspace.

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:16.910
Now let's go ahead and clone
some sample code from GitHub.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:21.830
Open the command palate by
selecting View, Find, Command.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:26.060
Type "Git Clone"
and click on it.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.702
Next, you'll be prompted to
type in the Git repository

00:02:28.702 --> 00:02:29.660
that you want to clone.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:37.130
After typing in a GitHub
URL, press Enter to continue.

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:38.780
Cloud Shell Editor
clones the files

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:41.870
for a sample application from
GitHub into a new folder,

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.240
allowing you to work across
multiple repositories

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:45.295
at the same time.

00:02:45.295 --> 00:02:46.670
Right-click on
the folder created

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.850
for your new repository, and
select Open as Workspace.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:56.570
You should see the list of code
files only for this repository.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:03:00.230
Here we have a simple Python
Flask web application.

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.510
Now let's try editing the
code and pushing the change

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:03.530
to GitHub.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.860
Open the main.py File and
replace "Hello World" with

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:07.490
"Hello Google."

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:16.290
Cloud Shell Editor will save
your changes automatically.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:17.905
On the left side
of your screen, you

00:03:17.905 --> 00:03:20.280
should see that the source
control icon has a blue circle

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.953
with the "1" inside it.

00:03:21.953 --> 00:03:23.370
This means that
we have a new code

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.860
change we may want to commit.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.680
Click on the source control icon
to open the source control Git

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.420
panel.

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.510
Now we see that we have
a code change in main.py

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.640
You can click on it to
open the [? DifView, ?]

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.120
which shows what you've changed
versus a previous commit.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.300
Hover over the main.py file in
the source control Git panel,

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:45.480
and click the plus by
it to save your changes.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.370
When you're ready to
check in your changes,

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:50.340
you can do so directly
within Cloud Shell Editor.

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:53.340
But first, you'll
need to configure Git.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:56.610
Select Terminal, New Terminal,
to open a new Cloud Shell

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:58.300
terminal window.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.690
From here, run the
following two commands

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:03.186
to set up Git from command line.

00:04:03.186 --> 00:04:05.676
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:18.089
Then, back in the source
control Git panel,

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:20.220
write a commit message
and click the checkmark

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:23.820
to check in your code change.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:26.460
Now you're ready to push
your change to GitHub.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:31.260
Back in the Cloud Shell
terminal window, run Git push.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.150
Type in your GitHub
username and password

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.858
to push your commit
to the master branch.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.310
Let's head over to GitHub to
verify that our change was

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:45.928
actually pushed successfully.

00:04:45.928 --> 00:04:47.970
Based on the information
about the latest commit,

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.560
it looks like we're good to go.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.280
You can also manage source
control from the Git status bar

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:54.810
at the bottom of your screen.

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:56.400
Right now we're on
the main branch,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.692
but we can click on it to
switch to a different branch,

00:04:58.692 --> 00:05:00.750
or create a new branch.

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:02.970
There's also a Synchronize
Changes button.

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:05.310
Synchronizing changes
pushes local changes

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.980
to your upstream branch,
and pulls remote changes

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:11.070
into your local repository.

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:12.595
With just a couple
of clicks, we're

00:05:12.595 --> 00:05:14.220
able to get started
with source control

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.502
and push a change to a Git repo.

00:05:16.502 --> 00:05:18.960
If you want to experience for
yourself all that Cloud Shell

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:23.045
Editor has to offer, just
visit ide.cloud.google.com,

00:05:23.045 --> 00:05:25.170
where we have interactive
tutorials to help you get

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:25.980
started.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:29.110
From more videos on Cloud Shell
Editor and a whole lot more,

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:30.780
make sure to subscribe
to the channel.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

